\fs20\
\s21 \li4233 \fi-2721 \fs28 \f0 \b \fs24 Declared In:\tab \f1 \b0 \fs28 Foundation/NSAutoreleasePool.h \
\fs20\
\fs20\
\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s10 \li1209 \fi-1007 \ri1007 \ql Class Description \
\fs14\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 The NSAutoreleasePool class is used to implement the Foundation Kit\'27s autorelease mechanism.  An NSAutoreleasePool object simply contains objects that have received an \b autorelease\b0  message, and when deallocated sends a \b release\b0  message to each of those objects.  An object can be put into the same pool several times, and receives a \b release\b0  message for each time it was put into the pool.  Thus, sending \b autorelease\b0  instead of \b release\b0  to an object extends the lifetime of that object until the pool itself is released or longer if the object is retained. This class specification contains information on fine-tuning your application\'27s handling of autorelease pools; see \'aaObject Ownership and Automatic Disposal\'ba in the introduction to the Foundation Kit for general information on using the autorelease mechanism.\
\fs14\
\fs28 You create an NSAutoreleasePool with the usual \b alloc\b0  and \b init\b0  messages, and dispose of it with \b release\b0  (an exception will be raised if you send \b autorelease \b0 or\b  retain\b0  to an autorelease pool).  An autorelease pool should always be released in the same context (invocation of a method or function, or body of a loop) that it was created.\
\fs14\
\fs28 NSAutoreleasePools are automatically created and destroyed in applications based on the Application Kit, so your code normally doesn\'27t have to worry about them.  (The Application Kit creates a pool at the beginning of the event loop and releases it at the end). There are two cases, though, where you might wish to create and destroy your own autorelease pools.  If you\'27re writing a program that\'27s not based on the Application Kit, such as a UNIX tool, there\'27s no built-in support for autorelease pools; you must create and destroy them yourself.  Also, if you write a loop that creates many temporary objects, you might wish to create an NSAutoreleasePool inside the loop to dispose of those objects before the next iteration.\
\fs14\
\fs28 Enabling the autorelease mechanism in a program that\'27s not based on the Application Kit is easy.  Many programs have a top-level loop where they do most of their work.  To enable the autorelease mechanism you create an NSAutoreleasePool at the beginning of this loop and release it at the end.  An \b autorelease\b0  message sent in the body of the loop automatically puts its receiver into this pool.  \
\fs14\
\fs28 Your \b main()\b0  function might look like this:\
\fs14\
\pard \s19 \li1915 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx2413 \tx2889 \tx3421 \tx3953 \tx4457 \tx4933 \tx7005 \tx7509 \tx8013 \tx8517 \tx9021 \tx9525 \tx10029 \tx10533 \tx11037 \tx11513 \f2 \fs24 void main()\
\fi0 \{\
\fi0    NSArray *args = [[NSProcessInfo processInfo] arguments];\
\fi0    unsigned count, limit = [args count];\
\fi0 \
\fi0    for (count = 1; count < limit; count++)\{\
\fi0       NSAutoreleasePool *pool =[[NSAutoreleasePool alloc] init];\
\fi0       NSString *fileContents;\
\fi0       NSString *fileName;\
\fi0 \
\fi0       fileName = [args objectAtIndex:count];\
\fi0       fileContents = [[NSString alloc] initWithContentsOfFile:fileName];\
\fi0       [fileContents autorelease];\
\fi0 \
\fi0       /* Process the file, creating and autoreleasing more objects. */\
\fi0 \
\fi0       [pool release];\
\fi0    \}\
\fi0 \
\fi0    /* Do whatever cleanup is needed. */\
\fi0 \
\fi0    exit (EXIT_SUCCESS);\
\fi0 \}\
\fs14\
\f1 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 This program processes files passed in on the command line. The \b for\b0  loop processes one file at a time. An NSAutoreleasePool is created at the beginning of this loop and released at the end. Therefore, any object sent an \b autorelease\b0  message inside the \b for\b0  loop, such as \b fileContents\b0 , is added to \b pool\b0 , and when \b pool\b0  is released at the end of the loop those objects are also released.\
\fs14\
\fs28 Similarly, NSAutoreleasePools can be created inside any loop, even in a program based on the Application Kit, that creates and releases many objects during each iteration.\
\fs14\
\fs26\
\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s57 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql Nesting Autorelease Pools\
\fs8\
\fs6\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 Autorelease pools can be nested allowing you to create them in any function or method. For example,  the \b main()\b0  function may create an autorelease pool and call another function that creates another autorelease pool.\
\fs14\
\fs28 Because an NSAutoreleasePool adds itself to the active pool when created, it doesn\'27t cause a memory leak in the face of an exception or other sudden transfer out of the current context.  For example, if an NSAutoreleasePool is created at the beginning of a loop and the program breaks out of the loop during execution, that pool is released by the application\'27s default pool (or whatever pool was active before it was created). In the case of a raised exception, the pool is released when the exception handler\'27s pool is released.  Thus it is not necessary to add exception handler code to release objects that were autoreleased.\
\fs14\
\fs26\
\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s57 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql Guaranteeing the Foundation Ownership Policy\
\fs8\
\fs6\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 By creating an NSAutoreleasePool instead of simply releasing objects, you extend the lifetime of temporary objects to the lifetime of that pool.  After an NSAutoreleasePool is deallocated, you should regard any object that was autoreleased while that pool was active as \'aadisposed of\'ba, and not send a message to that object or return it to the invoker of your method. \
\fs14\
\fs28 If you must use a temporary object beyond an autorelease context, you can do so by sending a \b retain\b0  message to the object within the context and then send it \b autorelease\b0  after the pool has been released as in:\
\fs14\
\pard \s19 \li1915 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx2413 \tx2889 \tx3421 \tx3953 \tx4457 \tx4933 \tx7005 \tx7509 \tx8013 \tx8517 \tx9021 \tx9525 \tx10029 \tx10533 \tx11037 \tx11513 \f2 \fs24 \f3 -\f2  findMatchingObject:anObject\
\fi0 \{\
\fi0     id match = nil;\
\fi0 \
\fi0     while (match == nil) \{\
\fi0         NSAutoreleasePool *subpool = [[NSAutoreleasePool alloc] init];\
\fi0     \
\fi0         /* Do a search that creates a lot of temporary objects. */\
\fi0         match = [self expensiveSearchForObject:anObject];\
\fi0 \
\fi0         if (match != nil) [match retain]; /* Keep match around. */\
\fi0 \
\fi0         [subpool release];\
\fi0     \}\
\fi0 \
\fi0     return [match autorelease];   /* Let match go and return it. */\
\fi0 \}\
\fs14\
\f1 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 By sending \b retain\b0  to \b match\b0  while \b subpool\b0  is in effect and sending \b autorelease\b0  to it after \b subpool\b0  has been released, \b match\b0  is effectively moved from \b subpool\b0  to the pool that was previously active.  This extends the lifetime of \b match\b0  and allows it to receive messages outside the loop and be returned to the invoker of \b findMatchingObject:\b0 .\
\fs14\
